{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they shall make an ark of acacia wood.</b> Regarding the making of the Tabernacle and all its vessels and the laws of their construction, the details of their laws are already found in the Talmud — to them one may not add and from them one may not subtract. There remains nothing for us to innovate concerning them, except in that upon which all the commentators agreed: to make in them allusions to hidden or revealed matters. For it is impossible to say that all these details were without intention. In this manner most authors proceeded, to make allusions in them, each according to his intellect — this one saying thus and that one thus. Even Rashi, whose every word is plain, nevertheless, wherever he found occasion to offer a derashah close to the plain sense, did not refrain from doing so. For in this very portion he explained the bronze altar as atoning for the brazen forehead, and likewise the twelve springs of water and seventy date-palms (Exodus 15:27) he explained as corresponding to the twelve tribes and seventy elders. Therefore I too shall not withhold from stating what a sound intellect may propose, to add somewhat to those allusions which the commentators spoke concerning them in these two portions, Terumah and Tetzaveh. And before I come to speak of them in detail, I shall state by way of a general principle regarding three chief vessels upon which a crown of gold was made: the ark, the table, and the altar. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 72b) that they correspond to three crowns: the crown of Torah, the crown of priesthood, and the crown of kingship. Look to the right and see: for these three vessels were divided in the matter of their cubits. The ark — all its cubits were broken (fractional). The altar — all its cubits were whole. And the table — some of them were broken and some were whole. Is this not a thing?" "And what seems close to me to say regarding this is as follows. For the Sages said regarding matters of ascent — look upward from yourself, for through this one will imagine in his soul that he is lacking perfection and has not reached the level of his fellow, and he will come to be envious of him and will add to the perfection of his wisdom, for the jealousy of scholars increases wisdom. But in the matters of this world — that is, all physical successes such as wealth and honor — one should look at someone who is below him, and through this cause he will rejoice in his lot, seeing that he has far more than many people. And as they said: one who prays should cast his eyes below and his heart above (Yevamot 105a) — this one who prays concerning the needs of soul and body should give his heart, which sees much wisdom and knowledge, toward one who is above him, and pray to HaShem to incline his heart toward the wisdom, so that he may reach the level of the whole. Thus Solomon asked nothing from HaShem except that He give him a hearing heart. And his eyes, which see the physical desires that are tangible, should cast them toward one who is below him in degree, and then he will rejoice in his lot and not seek excess of wealth, for Solomon said (Proverbs 30:8), ״רֵאשׁ וָעֹשֶׁר אַל תִּתֶּן לִי״. Therefore all the cubits of the ark were broken, to teach that every person should imagine in his soul as though he is lacking the perfection of wisdom and needs still to measure in order to fill what is missing — for every broken cubit indicates something lacking that needs to be filled. Thus: the essence of wisdom, from where shall it be found? (Job 28:12) — from one who considers himself as nothing and lacking, but not from one who is wise in his own eyes, who deceives himself by saying that he has already reached its ultimate end. For who is wise? One who learns from every person (Avot 4:1) — one who considers that he is lacking in wisdom and still needs to learn. And likewise regarding knowledge of the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, they said (Bechinat Olam 13:45): the ultimate knowledge is that we know we do not know You. Therefore all the cubits of the ark were broken — in length, in height, and in width — corresponding to three things that stand as obstacles before those who attain wisdom, and they are: the limited grasp of the one who perceives, the depth of that which is perceived, and the length of the preliminaries. For the height — that is the depth, for the matter is deep, who can find it out? That is the depth of the perceived. And the width corresponds to the limited grasp of the perceiver. And the length — that is the length of the preliminaries. And as it is written (Job 11:8), ״עֲמֻקָּה מִשְּׁאוֹל מַה תֵּדָע״, and it is written (Job 11:9), ״אֲרֻכָּה מֵאֶרֶץ מִדָּהּ וּרְחָבָה מִנִּי יָם״." "But the table, which alludes to the crown of kingship and to all the human successes which Israel merits from a high table — its cubits were some whole, for every discerning person rejoices in his lot and imagines in his soul as though he has everything and lacks nothing at all — as Jacob said, ״יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ (Genesis 33:11). However, some of them were broken, to teach that a person should not fulfill his desire entirely and should not give to matter every craving of his soul, but rather he must break his desire. For this reason the language of breaking is mentioned in connection with bread, as it is written (Genesis 43:2), ״שִׁבְרוּ לָנוּ מְעַט אֹכֶל״, and it is written (Leviticus 26:26), ״בְּשִׁבְרִי לָכֶם מַטֵּה לֶחֶם״. And this will be explained further in the vicinity, in the section concerning the table. But the incense altar — all its cubits were whole, and likewise the altar of burnt offering, for the subject of both of them is to complete the person who is lacking on account of his deeds. For everything that comes to atone for a person completes what is missing in him: the altar of burnt offering atones for the body of the sinner, through the offering of the bodies of living creatures, and the incense altar atones for the soul — and the spirit of Elohim ascends upward like the smoke of the incense — and therefore all the cubits of those were whole. And after this small introduction I shall explain in detail the matter of the Tabernacle and its vessels." "<b>And they shall make an ark.</b> Whereas concerning all the others it says ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״ (and you shall make), here it says ״וְעָשׂוּ״ (and they shall make) — the Torah said: let all come and engage in the ark, so that all may have a share in the Torah, for the crown of Torah is available to all. Another thing: because it says (Proverbs 3:18), ״עֵץ חַיִּים הִיא לַמַּחֲזִיקִים בָּהּ״ — ״לְלוֹמְדֶיהָ״ (to its learners) was not said, but rather ״לַמַּחֲזִיקִים בָּהּ״ (to those who hold fast to it) — an allusion to those who support the hands of those who learn Torah. And since not every person is suited for the learning of Torah, yet in any case there is an aspect in every person that he can have a share in the Torah — for even if he is not suited to learn, he can nevertheless support the hands of its learners, and it is accounted to him as though he himself learned it, like Issachar and Zebulun — therefore it says ״וְעָשׂוּ אֲרוֹן עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים״. For by virtue of the ark having in it wood, which alludes to ״כִּי עֵץ חַיִּים הִיא לַמַּחֲזִיקִים בָּהּ״, therefore all are able to make it. Therefore it says ״וְעָשׂוּ״ — for this is something that all are able to do. And it may be that this is the opinion of Rabbi Yohanan, who said in the tractate (Yoma 72b): from here we learn that the townspeople of a scholar are commanded to do his work for him." "And afterward He said, ״וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתוֹ זָהָב טָהוֹר מִבַּיִת וּמִחוּץ תְּצַפֶּנּוּ״. In the making of the acacia wood it says ״וְעָשׂוּ״, but in the overlaying of the gold it says ״וְצִפִּיתָ״ — for all are able to support the hands of those who learn Torah, which is alluded to by the wood. But not all are able to stand on the revealed and the hidden that are in Torah, which is alluded to by His saying ״מִבַּיִת וּמִחוּץ תְּצַפֶּנּוּ״, and likewise not all are within them and upon them as will be explained. And it seems reasonable to say that for this reason the ark itself was made of wood — for every tree produces fruit, and this alludes to the good reward that those who engage in Torah have, as it is said (Proverbs 27:18), ״נֹצֵר תְּאֵנָה יֹאכַל פִּרְיָהּ״ — and this is something that all yearn for, and therefore it says ״וְעָשׂוּ אֲרוֹן עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים״. But the overlaying of gold inside and out, which alludes to the essence of deed — not all people are equal in this, and it is designated for Moses and for men of distinction who are his equal; therefore it says ״וְצִפִּיתָ״. Now behold, the overlaying is mentioned twice in order to distinguish between inside and outside. For first it says ״וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתוֹ זָהָב טָהוֹר מִבַּיִת״, and afterward it says ״וּמִחוּץ תְּצַפֶּנּוּ״, and it did not mention pure gold for the outside. Though without doubt it was all of pure gold, nevertheless it did not mention pure gold in connection with the outside — for every craftsman's way is to adorn the vessel on the outside more than what he adorns on the inside, on account of those who see. Therefore there was no need to mention pure gold except in connection with the inside; but for the outside, I need not warn you that the overlaying should also be of gold, for it is a fortiori from the inside. And by way of allusion He implanted in their hearts that just as the vessels are, so should their makers be — for all who learn Torah need especially an admonition concerning the purity of the inner heart, which is not seen except by HaShem alone. But concerning their outward appearance they do not need such an admonition, for every person's way is to adorn himself in order to find favor in the eyes of others." "Another matter: <b>Therefore he did not mention \"pure gold\" with respect to the outside,</b> so that we learn from this that whatever a person does in order to adorn himself in the eyes of Elohim is certainly pure without doubt. But whatever he displays as outwardly pure is not definitely pure, for it may be that he does so on account of those who see, while his heart is not right with HaShem. Another matter: because the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts inward purity when one's thoughts are pure, but to purify oneself from the outside is a very difficult thing, for it is the way of the children of our people to judge every person strictly, for who is greater for us than Moshe, yet they fabricated things against him and suspected him of what was not in him — and if among cedars their shafted tongue's arrow fell, what can the moss of the wall do? Another matter: \"from inside and from outside\" alludes to the revealed and the hidden within the Torah, and it is known that the hidden possesses greater purity and holiness than the revealed; therefore he did not mention \"pure\" except with respect to \"from inside.\"" "And now there is room for me to explain further \"from inside and from outside,\" along the lines of what we find in tractate Mo'ed Katan (16b): a Rabbi decreed upon his students that they should not study in the marketplace, etc., and the Gemara asks there: but is it not written (Proverbs 1:20), ״חָכְמוֹת בַּחוּץ תָּרֹנָּה״? Rava said: whoever engages in Torah inside, his Torah proclaims about him from the outside. And according to this, there is greater perfection in Torah of the inside than in Torah that is studied outside; therefore he mentioned ״זָהָב טָהוֹר״ with respect to the inside, and did not mention this with respect to the outside. And perhaps it is for this reason that he placed \"inside\" before \"outside\" here, for he began with ״זָהָב טָהוֹר מִבַּיִת״, and from there it is learned also with respect to the outside. So too, whoever engages in Torah in the house — from there the matter will spread to the head of all streets."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {6 "The citation מוֹעֵד קָטָן (ט״ז.) is resolved as Mo'ed Katan 16b based on the standard pagination; the text reads ט״ז which could be folio 16."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:29.918518071Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}